#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079450} :activity/id #uuid "fa63b04f-e0ce-592f-b214-495b5c1b67a9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050566 :topic/id #uuid "546b628e-ba40-5207-8646-07c283c27604" :topic/math-academy-id 631} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079450 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079616 :tutorial/content "A **function** is a rule where *every* input value is assigned *precisely one* output value. \n\nAs an example, let's consider the rule defined by\n\n$$\nf(x) = x+3\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ can be any number from the **set** $\\{1,2,3,4\\}.$ A set is a collection of numbers we can use in our rule. \n\nLet's calculate the possible values of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =1+3=4 \\\\ f(2) & =2+3=5 \\\\ f(3) & =3+3=6 \\\\ f(4) & =4+3=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this rule using a **mapping diagram**, like the one shown below.\n\n![](images/47/4714001592222932934cb7edd5852542a2e990e6680daad85e2db88b9c7cef47.png)\n\nThis rule *is* a function because *every* input is mapped to *precisely one* output." :tutorial/id #uuid "88ebc343-41ab-500a-8668-451bbf36ec8a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1488 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "93acbf1d-7504-5269-982c-7785e36bfa3d" :step/math-academy-id 3581 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079452}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079451 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078527 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078142 :question/id #uuid "6d57991d-4cdb-546b-80c5-4df14f1b385d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1096"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cc2167e-d318-54d5-8b67-47afbc65f530" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510375}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Rule Given by a Mapping Diagram Is a Function"} :step/id #uuid "953349df-46b8-543b-a772-87c3e0ffd977" :step/math-academy-id 3585 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079454}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079452 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079694 :tutorial/content "Now let's consider the rule given by the following mapping diagram:\n\n![](images/b0/b02a511f8043bb7e39df2de082befa267f795948ea6c1cc1ef38eb9df4a8cb1d.png)\n\nThis rule is *not* a function because *at least one* input is assigned to *more than one* output. In this case, the input $x=1$ is mapped to *both* $2$ and $-2.$\n\nFinally, let's consider the rule given by the following mapping diagram:\n\n![](images/be/bea4ee8835a6111e105670863edede9450ad26dbfbea631454d5c8d6cd76a6a1.png)\n\nThis rule is *not* a function because *at least one* input is not assigned to an output. In this case, the input $0$ is not assigned an output." :tutorial/id #uuid "f58c4e80-86e3-5b65-99a3-2a16208231e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5913 :tutorial/title "Examples of Rules That Are Not Functions"} :step/id #uuid "4034d183-7b91-5f03-a1ca-b71ce2a3ea8c" :step/math-academy-id 36150 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079455}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079453 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078528 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078381 :question/id #uuid "279a2ac1-2577-5ec3-8eb4-8496650c6b54" :question/math-academy-id "e-9905"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b869d240-38f8-52c8-bb87-110d7ac616b8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510456}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Rule Given by a Table Is a Function"} :step/id #uuid "d65897aa-199d-5bb6-a05a-f5877fe63002" :step/math-academy-id 37242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079454 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079695 :tutorial/content "When a rule is represented as a table, we have a function if and only if all values in the *first row* are *distinct* (i.e., different).\n\nLet's consider some examples.\n\nThe rule defined by the following table is a function. Notice that all values in the first row are distinct. Every input value is assigned to exactly one output value.\n\n| **$x$** | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $0$ | $7$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, the rule defined by the following table is *not* a function. Notice that the first row contains the value $\\color{red}3$ twice. The input value $3$ is assigned to two output values ($0$ and $-1$).\n\n| **$x$** | $1$ | $2$ | $\\color{red}3$ | $4$ | $\\color{red}3$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $0$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $5$ |" :tutorial/id #uuid "f5d2e3d4-e985-565a-81f8-c8b8e93dc73d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6156 :tutorial/title "Rules Represented Using Tables"} :step/id #uuid "53c16c15-06ad-566b-bb58-7baf803dbf46" :step/math-academy-id 37341 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079453}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079455 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079696 :tutorial/content "So, we must remember that a rule is a function if and only if *every* input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\nIn particular, a rule is *not* a function in the following cases:\n\n- An input value is not assigned an output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with no arrow from it):\n\n![](images/bb/bb53865d210d631d78e5ce48aa96ecff99da8995936a085c789c3471ffb9e6a0.png)\n\n \n\n- An input value is assigned to more than one output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with more than one arrow from it):\n\n![](images/18/18831dc73e561faa69748fed111eb7e1024d35849fb6624594ae08d8c35301ee.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "f92a951f-7b57-51b4-b130-bf0d4c0da4a5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6161 :tutorial/title "A Summary"} :step/id #uuid "e2bd4f1a-fe61-532e-a324-4c924a280b8e" :step/math-academy-id 37364 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079451}}] :activity/title "Visual Representations of Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078655}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510497} :activity/id #uuid "02347787-e68b-52a4-afbc-1ad4be765c4f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050566 :topic/id #uuid "546b628e-ba40-5207-8646-07c283c27604" :topic/math-academy-id 631} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510497 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510375 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b452610-1a90-593f-89a8-1c4334f8692e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Yes, since both sets have the same number of elements" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509841} {:answer/id #uuid "a9162203-d658-5d42-9df6-3ec42dbfef2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Yes, since each output is related to at least one input" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510049} {:answer/id #uuid "c10dd595-c5fb-5d9b-b6ec-8500d9d83c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Yes, since each input is assigned to exactly one output" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510092} {:answer/id #uuid "cdc3a002-b6d3-5418-8801-26229653dddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Not enough information" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510122} {:answer/id #uuid "e849c16f-4c33-5f1d-9015-49309af959d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No, since both sets have the same number of elements" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510182}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510092} :answer-field/id #uuid "0509729a-0cf8-5abe-9a21-a913a1bfda28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2f8427f7-1892-52e0-9e03-33a7a2709f34" :question/math-academy-id "q-6728" :question/problem "![](images/99/998d9b7d5d9e342f58add32f80cddfcb6f01e255497f87376c0e39e01abb67ba.png)\n\nIs the above rule a function?" :question/worked-solution "A rule is a function if and only if *every* input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\nIn particular, a rule is *not* a function in the following cases:\n\n- An input value is not assigned to an output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with no arrow from it):\n\n![](images/bb/bb53865d210d631d78e5ce48aa96ecff99da8995936a085c789c3471ffb9e6a0.png)\n\n \n\n- An input value is assigned to more than one output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with more than one arrow from it):\n\n![](images/18/18831dc73e561faa69748fed111eb7e1024d35849fb6624594ae08d8c35301ee.png)\n\n \n\nIn this particular example, the rule *is* a function because *each* input value is assigned *precisely one* output value."} :step/id #uuid "40e19e3b-3dd3-5aaf-ade8-710dfe052f40" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510513}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510499 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079146 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cad4dd8-ca6d-55ab-9dc5-081de80e69a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Not enough information" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077655} {:answer/id #uuid "001094b5-522b-5aff-87d8-cdfd551294ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No, since there is at least one output with more than one input" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077656} {:answer/id #uuid "3024a1e1-3c8f-5f6d-ab79-bde1c13d5709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Yes, since each input is assigned to exactly one output" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077657} {:answer/id #uuid "4cd5fdb7-c2a9-539d-b5b1-60da0e4d051a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Yes, since each output is related to at least one input" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077658} {:answer/id #uuid "6008c3f2-d2aa-5fd1-96b6-5ae4ce87bd88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No, since both sets have different numbers of elements" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077659}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186077657} :answer-field/id #uuid "41d9b076-e24e-5725-bd8f-2293f79ac91a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "77dee980-a89b-544a-9fab-994ab35aaa5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-60621" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aaba52792efd52cae40bed740b50f3f92f54e0f13436c16ffc8358ff8200cd76.png)\n\nIs the above rule a function?" :question/worked-solution "A rule is a function if and only if *every* input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\nIn particular, a rule is *not* a function in the following cases:\n\n- An input value is not assigned to an output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with no arrow from it):\n\n![](images/bb/bb53865d210d631d78e5ce48aa96ecff99da8995936a085c789c3471ffb9e6a0.png)\n\n \n\n- An input value is assigned to more than one output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with more than one arrow from it):\n\n![](images/18/18831dc73e561faa69748fed111eb7e1024d35849fb6624594ae08d8c35301ee.png)\n\n \n\nIn this particular example, the rule *is* a function because *each* input value is assigned *precisely one* output value."} :step/id #uuid "44c9b8f5-0dec-5758-9859-bd384b4e4b61"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510513 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510456 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2327a800-cbca-5988-8f96-dcae5bdc00c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-6,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509762} {:answer/id #uuid "2b22e163-0fc6-5bd7-bfc7-4b03d09bad8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509777} {:answer/id #uuid "3551050d-f232-5fde-b085-deb54a7b7231" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,28)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509803} {:answer/id #uuid "74addcf0-8125-5f4d-a238-5f72f1ddecb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(4,12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509935} {:answer/id #uuid "b145389b-1dbe-5fde-882c-d9f9182c3df8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(9,26)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510069}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509777} :answer-field/id #uuid "61917ff0-105b-5bca-9ac2-b0132beab63d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d26e72e3-99fa-5024-9c2a-cf9b61a0b079" :question/math-academy-id "q-31655" :question/problem "Which missing $xy$-pair would make the following rule a function?\n\n| **$x$** | $-6$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | ? | $4$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $30$ | $26$ | $1$ | ? | $3$ | $12$ | $28$ |" :question/worked-solution "A rule is a function if and only if every input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\n- If we substitute the $xy$-pair $(3,2)$ into our table, we get a function.\n\n| **$x$** | $-6$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $4$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $30$ | $26$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $12$ | $28$ |\n\nNotice that all values in the first row are *distinct* (i.e., different). Every input value is assigned to *precisely one* output value.\n\n- None of the other $xy$-pairs gives rules that are functions. So, for example, substituting the $xy$-pair $(4,12)$ gives a rule that is *not* a function. \n\n| **$x$** | $-6$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $4$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $30$ | $26$ | $1$ | $12$ | $3$ | $12$ | $28$ |\n\nNotice that the first row contains the value $x=4$ twice. The input value $4$ is assigned to *more than one* output value ($3$ and $12$)."} :step/id #uuid "6bc8b0db-4843-5d1a-9bdd-1c1077ab9b40" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510499}}] :activity/title "Visual Representations of Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509677}]}
